window.docContents[229].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Bounded-Solution-Operators--Regularity-as-a-bounded-operator":{"contents":"An existence theorem may say that every forcing  has a solution with two\nadditional weak derivatives.  For linear PDE, one wants more: the chosen\nsolution should depend linearly and continuously on .  Uniqueness of the\nunderlying weak Dirichlet problem makes the choice canonical, and the closed\ngraph theorem supplies continuity once qualitative regularity has been\nestablished.\n\nThe resulting operators  and\n solve the principal\ndivergence-form Dirichlet problem, under the boundary-chart, coefficient, and\nellipticity hypotheses of the corresponding global regularity theorems.\n\nFor an open domain and a bounded weak Dirichlet solver,\nthe higher-order lift condition means that every  forcing has an\n representative whose base coordinate is the weak solution\n. Concrete elliptic regularity theorems prove this predicate, and the\ngeneric functional-analytic construction takes it as input.\n\nThe predicate is purely qualitative: it asserts the existence of an \nrepresentative for each datum and says nothing about how that representative\ndepends on the datum. Linearity and continuity are consequences, the former\nbecause a Sobolev field on an open set is determined by its base coordinate,\nthe latter by the closed graph theorem.\n\nLet  be open, let\n be a bounded linear weak solver, and\nsuppose every  datum has an  representative whose base\ncoordinate is the weak solution . Uniqueness of Sobolev fields from\ntheir base coordinate makes this qualitative lift linear. Its graph is\nclosed because both the Sobolev base-coordinate map and the weak solver are\ncontinuous. The closed graph theorem therefore gives a canonical bounded\nlinear mapwhose base coordinate is exactly the original weak solution and whose norm\nobeys the usual operator-norm estimate. The construction uses nothing about\nthe coefficients, which enter only through the lift hypothesis.\n\nThe first concrete instance takes . The global  theorem supplies\nthe qualitative lift for the principal divergence-form weak solver, and the\ngeneric construction turns it into a bounded operator from  to\n.\n\nUnder the hypotheses of the global  theorem, the canonical weak\nDirichlet solver lifts to a bounded linear mapIts value is the unique  representative of the weak solution.\n\nContinuity of a linear map between normed spaces is the same as an\noperator-norm inequality, so the estimate is a restatement of boundedness.\n\nFor every , the canonical solution satisfiesThe constant is independent of the forcing datum.\n\nThe regularity and uniqueness argument has already bundled the solver as a\ncontinuous linear map . The conclusion is\nexactly the defining operator-norm inequality\n.\n\nThe all-orders theorem supplies the qualitative lift for every , under the\ncorrespondingly stronger chart and coefficient hypotheses.\n\nFor every , the all-orders qualitative theorem and uniqueness\nof the weak solution produce a bounded linear mapunder the corresponding chart, coefficient, and ellipticity assumptions.\nThe weak  solver uses the canonical order-one restriction of the\n coefficient bundle, while the unreduced bundle supplies\nthe derivatives used by the regularity theorem. The base-coordinate identity\ncharacterizes the result without unfolding either restriction or the\nclosed-graph construction.\n\nThe all-orders solution map satisfies, for every ,\n\nQualitative regularity gives a unique higher-order representative for each\nforcing.  Linearity follows from uniqueness of the base weak solution, and a\nclosed-graph argument upgrades the resulting linear map to a continuous\nlinear map.  Its standard operator-norm inequality is the asserted estimate.\n\nEvolution problems apply elliptic regularity at each time to a residual that\ndepends on time. Given a Bochner trajectory of weak solutions, the stationary\noperator can be applied at almost every , and the questions are\nmeasurability of the result and control of its norm.\n\nLet the right-hand side belong to \nand the given weak-solution trajectory belong to .\nAssume the generic higher-order solver is available and the weak-solution\nidentity holds almost everywhere. Then there exists a trajectory in\n whose base coordinate\nagrees almost everywhere with the original weak solution and which obeys the\noperator-norm estimate. The  memberships are separate hypotheses\nbecause the time measure may be infinite.\n\nApply the stationary higher-order solution operator at almost every time.\nThe weak-solution identity and uniqueness identify its base coordinate with\nthe original trajectory. Measurability of the lifted map and the stationary\noperator-norm bound give the Bochner representative, its essential-supremum\nestimate, and the asserted almost-everywhere compatibility.\n\nThe trajectory statement is an existence result. Behind it stands an operator:\nthe stationary solution operator lifts to a bounded linear map between Bochner\nspaces, which is the object that the higher-regularity argument for the wave\nequation composes with.\n\nFor any measure  on time, the generic higher-order solver acts on\nBochner trajectories as a bounded linear mapIt agrees almost everywhere with pointwise application of the stationary\nsolver, and its Sobolev base coordinate is almost everywhere the\ncorresponding weak solution. If  almost\neverywhere, then the lifted trajectory belongs to\n and satisfiesalmost everywhere.\n\nLift the stationary continuous linear map pointwise to Bochner .\nThe standard functorial construction gives boundedness and almost-everywhere\nagreement with pointwise application.  Composing with the base-coordinate\nmap yields the weak solution identity.  Finally, the stationary operator-norm\nbound applied almost everywhere preserves the assumed  envelope.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Elliptic Regularity and Solution Operators\u0009Bounded Solution Operators","header":"5.6.2.1. Regularity as a bounded operator","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Bounded-Solution-Operators--Regularity-as-a-bounded-operator"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Passing-nonlinear-terms-to-the-limit":{"contents":"Compactness becomes useful for a nonlinear PDE only after one can identify\nthe limit of its nonlinear terms. The basic limit passages that follow an\nAubin–Lions argument concern bilinear products, trilinear forms, and duality\npairings.\n\nFor scalar functions, the issue is already visible in squaring. The sequence\n, indexed by positive integers, converges weakly to zero in\n, but its square has integral  for every . A weak\nlimit therefore cannot simply be substituted into a nonlinear term.\nStrong  convergence repairs this:\n\n\n\nHere we used  and Cauchy–Schwarz. The same\nsplitting works for any continuous bilinear map. For a PDE application, the\ncompactness must occur in the norms needed by its particular nonlinearity.\n\nLet  be a continuous bilinear map, and suppose\n satisfyIf  in  and\n in , thenIn particular, taking  and , strong  convergence implies\n strongly in . This passes quadratic terms to the limit once compactness gives strong\n convergence.\n\nSplit the difference asThe Bochner Hölder estimate bounds its  norm byBoth terms tend to zero. Formally, the Hölder operation is itself a\ncontinuous bilinear map between the three Bochner spaces, so the result is\nalso an immediate continuity argument. The quadratic  statement is the\nspecialization , , and .\n\nProduct limits identify quadratic terms once strong convergence is known.\nThe convective terms of fluid equations are trilinear, and their first\nproperty is algebraic rather than topological: skew-symmetry in the last two\narguments makes the nonlinearity invisible to the energy identity and yields\na usable difference formula for two states.\n\nLet  be a continuous trilinear form that\nis skew in its last two arguments:Then , and in particular . Thus a nonlinear\nevolution equation tested against its state has the same energy balance as\nits linear coercive part. The same cancellation gives the difference\nidentityIf , the right-hand side is bounded by\n. This is the abstract algebra behind convective energy\ncancellation and many weak-uniqueness estimates. The same product bound shows\nthat the dual-valued quadratic map  is Lipschitz on\nbounded subsets of .\n\nSkew-symmetry with  gives , hence the value is\nzero over . Substituting this identity removes the cubic term\nfrom the tested evolution equation. For the difference formula, write\n with , expand by trilinearity, and cancel the terms\n and . The product bound applied to\n gives the quantitative estimate.\n\nCancellation controls the energy of the approximations; passing the\ntrilinear term itself to the limit is a separate question. Aubin–Lions gives\ncompactness in , and the limit passage needs strong convergence\nof two factors in norms in which the trilinear form is continuous.\n\nLet  be a continuous trilinear form.\nSupposewith the first two convergences strong and the weakly convergent family\n uniformly bounded. ThenIn particular, strong convergence of  and bounded weak convergence of\n identify the quadratic expression . This is the\nabstract strong–strong–weak passage used after compactness has supplied the\nstrong factors.\n\nThe functionals  converge strongly in  by continuity\nof the first two slots. SplitUniform boundedness of  sends the first term to zero, while weak\nconvergence tested against the fixed functional  sends the\nsecond term to zero.\n\nThe same weak–strong principle is needed in Bochner form for the duality\npairing between weakly convergent dual derivatives  and strongly\nconvergent states, the pairing that appears when an energy identity is\npassed to the limit. The pointwise Riesz map reduces it to a Hilbert-space\ninner product of a weakly convergent factor with a strongly convergent one.\n\nLet  be a real Hilbert space. Suppose  weakly in\n, the Riesz representatives of  are uniformly bounded,\nand  strongly in . ThenThe explicit bound is retained in the formal statement because it is valid\nfor arbitrary filters; ordinary weakly convergent sequences supply that bound\nautomatically.\n\nUse the pointwise Riesz map to rewrite each integral as the real\n inner product of the Riesz representative of  with .\nThe generic weak–strong inner-product theorem then passes to the limit:\nthe fixed-test term follows from weak convergence, and the varying-test error\nis controlled by the uniform norm bound times .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Aubin–Lions Compactness","header":"6.3.1.10.5. Passing nonlinear terms to the limit","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Passing-nonlinear-terms-to-the-limit"}});